<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Aegium</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Aegium</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Achaean city on the Gulf of Corinth where the assembly of the Achaean League met at the sanctuary of Zeus Homarios.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Aegium lay on the southern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a>, one of the twelve Achaean cities of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. It grew in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> on a terrace above a harbour; myth said <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a> gathered the Greek chiefs there before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> Homarios near Aegium was the religious centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaeans-achaea">Achaeans</a>. After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League</a> was refounded in 280 BCE, Aegium expelled its Macedonian garrison in 275 BCE and joined; the league's regular assemblies met there. The town lived from farming and the coastal trade of the gulf.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philopoemen">Philopoemen</a> moved the meetings in rotation among member cities in the 180s BCE, reducing Aegium's role. It passed under Roman control in 146 BCE and continues as the modern town of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barony-of-vostitsa">Aigio</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/aegium.jpg" alt="Aegium" width="320" height="175"><figcaption>Image: Badseed · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Towns_of_ancient_Achaia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aegium</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Zeus Homarios</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Achaean Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aigion, Aigio</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-473&amp;focus=aegium" aria-label="Aegium on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5229 804 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Aegium lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5549 1060L5549 1060"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5549 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5549 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5549 1060h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-473&amp;focus=aegium">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

